          <a href="#alternatives" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Against the Alternatives</a>
          <a href="#examples" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Three Real Decks</a>
          <a href="#where-to-spend" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Where to Spend</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The number worth calculating is not what stripping costs. It is what stripping costs against the two alternatives nobody prices properly: recoating without stripping, and replacing the deck boards entirely.
        </p>

        <div id="diy-materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. DIY Material Costs, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            The consumables for a deck strip are genuinely inexpensive, and seeing them itemised explains why the DIY route is so much cheaper than a quote even after you value your own time at something.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Item</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Cost</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Covers</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per sq ft</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Percarbonate stripper (powder)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$25-35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500-750 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.04-0.07</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Caustic stripper (liquid)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$30-45 per gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-150 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.20-0.45</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Caustic gel (for paint)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$40-60 per gal</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75-100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.40-0.80</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Brightener (concentrate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20-30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">500-750 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.03-0.06</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Scrapers and blades</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20-35</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">-</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Plastic sheeting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$15-25</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">-</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gloves, eye protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20-30</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Whole job</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">-</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Add it up for a typical 550 square foot treated surface with one coat of solid stain: roughly 7 gallons of caustic stripper at $35 is $245, brightener around $30, and $70 of sundries. Call it $345, or about $0.63 a square foot of treated surface. On a worn semi-transparent stain using a powder percarbonate product, the same deck is closer to $120 all in, or $0.22.
          </p>

          <p>
            That range, roughly $0.20 to $0.60 per square foot of treated surface, covers most DIY deck strips. The top end is a painted deck needing gel and two applications, which can reach $0.90. Our coverage guide on how much deck stripper you need works out the quantities behind these figures.
          </p>

          <p>
            Note that the treated surface is not the floor area. Railings, stairs and fascia typically add about 40 percent, so a deck described as 320 square feet is usually 450 to 550 square feet of surface to strip. Costing from the floor figure understates materials by the same margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            One cost people forget entirely: the finish. Stripping is preparation, and you still have to buy stain or paint afterwards, which on a mid-size deck is typically another $100 to $250 depending on product and coats. The strip is rarely the largest line on the total refinishing bill.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="tools" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Tools and Rental
          </h2>

          <p>
            The tool question is really the pressure washer question, and it is the one place a DIY strip can quietly become expensive.
          </p>

          <p>
            A pressure washer rental runs somewhere around $60 to $100 a day, or $200 to $350 for a week, depending on machine and region. For a one-weekend job on a small deck, renting is clearly right. For a large deck spread over two weekends, the rental cost starts approaching the price of a modest electric machine you would keep.
          </p>

          <p>
            An electric pressure washer in the 1,500 to 2,000 PSI range costs roughly $150 to $300 and is, for deck work specifically, the better tool than a petrol machine. Deck timber is damaged above about 1,500 PSI and cedar below that, so the extra power of a petrol unit is not an advantage here and is actively a risk. Our comparison of electric and gas pressure washers covers the trade in general terms.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rest of the tools are cheap and worth buying properly. A decent scraper with replaceable blades beats a bent putty knife by a wide margin, because a sharp edge lifts softened finish while a dull one tears wood fibres and creates the fuzzy surface that then needs sanding. Budget $20 to $35 and keep a file to dress the edge as you work.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are going the sanding route on flat boards, a random orbit sander is $60 to $150 and a floor sander rental is $50 to $80 a day. Sanding discs are a real consumable on a deck strip; old finish clogs abrasive quickly and you will use far more than you expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not skip the protective equipment on cost grounds. Chemical-resistant gauntlet gloves, sealed eye protection and a cheap respirator for sanding come to perhaps $40 and prevent the kind of injury that makes the whole calculation irrelevant.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="professional" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What a Professional Charges, and Why
          </h2>

          <p>
            Quotes vary widely by region and by how the job is specified, but the structure is consistent enough to sanity-check what you are given.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Service</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical Range per sq ft</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">On a 400 sq ft Deck</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean and brighten only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$0.75-1.50</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$300-600</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strip, brighten, no finish</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1.50-3.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$600-1,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strip and re-stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$2.50-5.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,000-2,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Strip paint and re-stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$4.00-7.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,600-2,800</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sand and re-stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$3.00-6.00</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$1,200-2,400</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Replace boards and stain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$20-45</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$8,000-18,000</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Most contractors quote on floor square footage while pricing in the railings, which is why two quotes for the same deck can differ so much: one may have counted an elaborate spindle railing properly and the other not. Ask explicitly whether the price includes railings, stairs and fascia, and whether it includes the brightener step.
          </p>

          <p>
            The gap between the $0.20 to $0.60 of materials and the $2.50 to $5.00 of a strip-and-stain quote is almost entirely labour, and it is not padding. Stripping a deck properly is slow, physical, chemical work done kneeling, and a painted deck involves scraping every square foot by hand. A 400 square foot deck with railings is typically two to three days of work for one person.
          </p>

          <p>
            Get the specification in writing, particularly on three points: whether a brightener or neutraliser step is included after a caustic strip, how many coats of finish, and what happens if boards are found to be rotten once the finish is off. That last one is the commonest source of a change order, because solid stain and paint are often hiding exactly that.
          </p>

          <p>
            Be wary of a quote that is dramatically below the range. The usual corners are skipping the brightener, pressure washing at damaging pressure to save chemical dwell time, and applying finish before the wood has dried. All three look fine on handover day and fail within a season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drivers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What Actually Drives the Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            In rough order of impact, so you can predict where your own deck will land.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Finish Being Removed</h3>

          <p>
            By far the largest factor. Worn semi-transparent stain is the cheap case; paint is the expensive one, at roughly three to four times the material cost and considerably more than that in labour. Solid stain sits in between and closer to paint than people expect.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Railings and Complexity</h3>

          <p>
            A deck with 60 feet of turned spindle railing has vastly more treatable surface and far slower access than the same deck with a simple cable or metal baluster rail. This is the factor most likely to make two similar-sized decks quote differently.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coat Count</h3>

          <p>
            Each additional historic coat means another application and another pass of scraping. Three coats of solid stain over fifteen years can double the job against a single coat, and it is invisible until someone does a test patch.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wood Condition</h3>

          <p>
            Old, open-grained, weathered wood drinks more product and holds finish deeper in the grain. It also raises the chance of finding boards that need replacing, which is a separate cost that frequently arrives mid-project.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Access and Site Protection</h3>

          <p>
            A ground-level deck over lawn is straightforward. A raised deck over a patio, or one surrounded by mature planting, needs significant sheeting and runoff management, and that is real time on a professional quote.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Cost Multipliers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3-4x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Paint vs semi-transparent stain</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+40%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Surface added by rails and stairs</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Three coats vs one</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~90%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Of a pro quote that is labour</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Stripping Against the Alternatives
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the comparison that actually decides what you should do, and stripping is not automatically the answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean and recoat, without stripping, is dramatically cheaper and is the right answer more often than people assume. If the existing finish is a semi-transparent stain that has faded rather than failed, a cleaner, a brightener and a fresh coat costs a fraction of a strip and produces a good result. Stripping is required when you are changing finish type, when the existing film is peeling, or when you are going lighter.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sanding competes directly on flat boards and small decks, with no runoff to manage. It loses badly on railings and spindles. The hybrid, sanding the field and stripping the rails, is what many experienced refinishers actually do and is often the cheapest total route on a mid-size deck.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacing the deck boards is the alternative worth pricing honestly on a painted deck. Decking material runs roughly $3 to $8 a square foot for pressure-treated or cedar, considerably more for hardwood or composite. On a 400 square foot deck that is $1,200 to $3,200 in material, against a professional strip-and-repaint quote of $1,600 to $2,800 and a DIY effort of two to three weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            That comparison surprises people, and it is why the test patch matters before committing. If the boards under the paint turn out to be soft, replacement is clearly correct. If they are sound, stripping preserves a deck that is otherwise fine and is the better value.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more option that is legitimate and rarely mentioned: recoat with solid stain or paint over the existing sound film, and defer the strip. If adhesion is good over most of the deck, scraping the failed areas, spot-priming and recoating buys five or more years for a fraction of the cost. It postpones rather than solves, but postponing cheaply is a valid choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Three Real Decks, Costed
          </h2>

          <p>
            Materials only for the DIY column, with the professional range for the same job.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck 1: 12 x 14, Worn Semi-Transparent Stain</h3>

          <p>
            Treated surface about 270 square feet including railings and three steps. DIY: a powder percarbonate stripper at $30, brightener at $25, sundries at $50, plus a $70 weekend pressure washer rental. About $175, or $0.65 a square foot all in. Professional strip and brighten: $400 to $800. A clear DIY job for a single weekend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck 2: 16 x 20, One Coat of Solid Stain</h3>

          <p>
            Treated surface about 550 square feet. DIY: roughly 7 gallons of caustic stripper at $245, brightener at $30, sundries at $70, rental at $100. About $445, or $0.81 a square foot. Add stain at roughly $200. Professional strip and re-stain: $1,000 to $2,000 on the floor area. Two full weekends of DIY work.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Deck 3: 20 x 24, Three Coats of Paint</h3>

          <p>
            Treated surface about 800 square feet. DIY: roughly 18 gallons of caustic gel at around $50 a gallon is $900, brightener $50, sundries $100, week-long rental $250. About $1,300 in materials alone, plus two to three weekends of hard scraping. Professional strip and repaint: $3,200 to $5,600. New pressure-treated boards: roughly $2,400 to $3,200 in material. This is the deck where replacement deserves serious consideration.
          </p>

          <p>
            The pattern across the three is the point. On a lightly stained deck, DIY stripping is cheap and obviously worthwhile. On a heavily painted deck, DIY materials alone approach the cost of new boards, and the labour is substantial. The finish on the deck, not its size, is what determines whether this is a cheap job or a large one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-to-spend" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Where to Spend and Where to Save
          </h2>

          <p>
            Given a fixed budget, some line items return far more than others.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on the right stripper for your finish. A cheap percarbonate product on a solid-stained deck produces patchy removal and forces a second full application, costing more in total than buying the correct caustic gel at the outset. This is the single most common false economy in the job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on the brightener. It is inexpensive relative to the stripper and it directly determines how evenly the new finish takes after a caustic strip. Skipping it to save $30 on a job where you are about to spend $200 on stain is poor arithmetic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spend on scrapers and blades. A sharp edge lifts softened finish; a dull one tears fibres and creates a surface that needs sanding. Blades are a consumable here, not a one-time purchase.
          </p>

          <p>
            Save on the pressure washer. You need less pressure than you think, an inexpensive electric machine in the 1,500 PSI range is the correct tool for deck work, and renting for a single weekend is entirely reasonable. Do not buy a petrol machine for this job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Save by doing the preparation and letting a professional do the finish, or the reverse, if the budget is tight. Splitting the job is unusual but perfectly workable, and stripping is the part where labour is cheapest to substitute with your own time.
          </p>

          <p>
            And save, potentially a great deal, by spending ten minutes on a test patch before you buy anything. It tells you the coat count, the finish type and the condition of the wood underneath, which between them determine whether this is a $175 weekend or a $1,300 fortnight.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Costing From Floor Area Instead of Treated Surface</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Railings, stairs and fascia typically add about 40 percent to the area you actually treat, so a deck described as 320 square feet is usually 450 to 550 square feet of work. Costing from the floor figure understates both materials and time by the same margin, and it is why DIY budgets come up short.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the Cheap Stripper for a Solid or Painted Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A mild percarbonate product on a cured acrylic film gives patchy removal and forces a second full application, costing more in total than the correct caustic gel would have. This is the most common false economy in the job, and it also wastes a weekend.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the Finish in the Budget</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stripping is preparation, not the finished job. Stain or paint afterwards adds roughly $100 to $250 on a mid-size deck. The strip is rarely the largest line on the total refinishing bill, and budgeting for it alone leaves you with a bare deck and no money.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Accepting a Quote Without Asking What Is Included</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Contractors often quote on floor square footage while pricing railings differently, which is why two quotes for one deck can diverge wildly. Ask specifically whether railings, stairs and fascia are included, whether the brightener step is, and what happens if rotten boards are found once the finish is off.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Pricing Board Replacement on a Painted Deck</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                On a large painted deck, DIY materials alone can approach the cost of new decking, before two or three weekends of scraping. Pressure-treated or cedar runs roughly $3 to $8 a square foot. If a test patch reveals soft boards underneath, replacement is clearly the better value.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to strip a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                DIY materials run roughly $0.20 to $0.60 per square foot of treated surface, so about $120 to $350 on a typical mid-size deck, rising towards $0.90 for a painted one. Professional strip-and-stain typically runs $2.50 to $5.00 per square foot, or $1,000 to $2,000 on a 400 square foot deck.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are professional deck stripping quotes so much higher than materials?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because roughly 90 percent of the price is labour, and it is not padding. Stripping is slow, physical, chemical work done kneeling, and a painted deck requires scraping every square foot by hand. A 400 square foot deck with railings is typically two to three days of work for one person.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it cheaper to strip a deck or replace the boards?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a lightly stained deck, stripping is far cheaper and obviously right. On a heavily painted deck the comparison is genuinely close: DIY stripper alone can run over $1,000 on a large deck, while pressure-treated or cedar decking is roughly $3 to $8 a square foot. Do a test patch first, because soft boards settle it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need to rent a pressure washer to strip a deck?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You need one, but renting versus buying depends on scale. A rental is $60 to $100 a day, and an electric machine in the 1,500 to 2,000 PSI range costs $150 to $300 and is the correct tool for decking. Deck timber is damaged above about 1,500 PSI, so a powerful petrol machine is a liability here, not an advantage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does deck brightener add to the cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Very little. It is almost always sold as a concentrate or powder covering 500 to 750 square feet for $20 to $30, so around $0.03 to $0.06 per square foot. After a caustic strip it is a required step, since the wood is left alkaline and darkened, and skipping it directly affects how the new finish takes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does stripping cost more than just cleaning and recoating?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Substantially, several times over. If the existing finish is a semi-transparent stain that has faded rather than peeled, cleaning, brightening and recoating is a fraction of the cost and gives a good result. Stripping is necessary when you are changing finish type, going lighter, or the existing film is peeling.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What makes one deck cost twice as much to strip as another the same size?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The finish, first: paint costs three to four times what a worn semi-transparent stain does. Then railing complexity, since turned spindles have far more surface and slower access than metal balusters. Then coat count, which is invisible until someone strips a test patch, and can double the job on its own.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

